the u.n secretary general came to the  ukrainian port city of odessa to see for  himself the grain being loaded onto  ships  the deal between ukraine and russia to  open the waters to cargo ships and to  relieve the global food crisis is pretty  much the only diplomatic success in this  six-month war so far  but there is now another crisis and it's  getting worse by the day the fate of  europe's largest nuclear power plant  near zaparija which has been occupied by  russian forces since march  fighting around the site threatens a  nuclear disaster and a blame game is at  play  both ukraine and russia blame each other  for bombing the zaparisian nuclear power  plant what do you think the truth is and  today there are reports that russia  is planning to  divert the electricity from the power  plant to the russian grid with that in  mind what's your message for moscow  if we demilitarize as we propose  the plant  the problem will be solved  zaporizia is ukrainian electricity and  it's necessary especially during the  winter for the ukrainian people and  these  principles must be fully respected  russia has rejected the idea of  demilitarization and has claimed that  the power plant is actually safer with  russian forces there  ukrainians living nearby don't feel that  way  watching  the french president emmanuel macron  spoke with vladimir putin on the phone  this afternoon to try and find a  breakthrough but all attempts to get  independent inspectors onto the site  have so far failed  recent satellite images taken in the  last few days show no signs of major  shelling or damage  russia's control of the power plant puts  putin in a powerful position he's  unlikely to give it up easily  alistair model sky news in odessa
 

